<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Desmond Tutu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Archbishop Desmond Tutu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Desmond Tutu was a South African Anglican cleric and anti-apartheid campaigner, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> from 1986 to 1996. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1984 for his non-violent opposition to apartheid. After 1994 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, which heard testimony from victims and perpetrators of apartheid-era abuses, and he popularised the phrase &quot;Rainbow Nation&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu.jpg" alt="Desmond Tutu" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benny Gool · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishop-Tutu-medium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
